-- Q3 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A researcher studying an enzyme adds a molecule that binds the active site in a manner mutually exclusive with substrate. When she plots initial velocity against substrate concentration at a fixed inhibitor concentration, she expects that driving \([S]\) to saturation should still overcome the inhibitor. Compared with the uninhibited reaction, how would this competitive inhibitor most likely affect the enzyme''s \(V_{max}\) and apparent \(K_m\)?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(V_{max}\\) decreases, and the apparent \\(K_m\\) is unchanged."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Both \\(V_{max}\\) and the apparent \\(K_m\\) decrease."}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(V_{max}\\) is unchanged, and the apparent \\(K_m\\) increases."}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(V_{max}\\) is unchanged, and the apparent \\(K_m\\) decreases."}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because a competitive inhibitor binds the same active site as the substrate, so saturating substrate outcompetes the inhibitor and full velocity is still reached, while more substrate is needed to reach half-maximal velocity. The source states that in reversible competitive inhibition, ''substrate (S) and inhibitor (I) both bind to the same site on the enzyme... they compete for the active site and bind in a mutually exclusive fashion'' (line 27809). Because ''as S increases to infinity, all E is converted to ES... v = VM. VM is not changed. However, the apparent KM, KMapp, will change'' (lines 27829-27831), and explicitly ''In the presence of I, VM does not change, but KM appears to increase'' (line 27845). (Choice A) is wrong: a decreased \(V_{max}\) with unchanged \(K_m\) is the noncompetitive signature, where the inhibitor binds both E and ES so ''the Km is unchanged, and Vm decreases'' (line 28254), not the competitive pattern. (Choice B) is wrong: both parameters decreasing is the uncompetitive signature, where the inhibitor ''binds only to ES and not free E'' (line 28099) so that ''both Vm and Km decrease'' (line 28121); a competitive inhibitor does not lower \(V_{max}\). (Choice C) is correct: because the inhibitor is displaced at saturating substrate, \(V_{max}\) is unchanged, and because binding of I to free E shifts E + S toward free E, ''it would increase the KMapp'' (lines 27831-27833), consistent with ''VM does not change, but KM appears to increase'' (line 27845). (Choice D) is wrong: no reversible inhibitor produces an unchanged \(V_{max}\) with a decreased \(K_m\); competitive inhibition raises the apparent \(K_m\) rather than lowering it, since ''KMapp = KM(1+I/Kis)'' is greater than \(K_m\) (line 27869-27870). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that competitive inhibitors bind the active site, leave \(V_{max}\) unchanged, and raise the apparent \(K_m\).', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
