-- Q4 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A researcher assays an enzyme in the presence of a fixed concentration of a competitive inhibitor and then progressively raises the substrate concentration to a very high, saturating level. Assuming the inhibitor is reversible, the maximum velocity attainable under these saturating conditions would most likely be expected to:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "fall to zero, because the inhibitor prevents the enzyme from turning over any substrate."}, {"label": "B", "text": "be reached only at an apparent \\(K_m\\) that has dropped below its uninhibited value."}, {"label": "C", "text": "remain permanently below the uninhibited \\(V_{max}\\) even at infinite substrate."}, {"label": "D", "text": "still reach the original uninhibited \\(V_{max}\\) as excess substrate outcompetes the inhibitor."}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because a competitive inhibitor and the substrate bind mutually exclusively at the same active site, so flooding the enzyme with substrate outcompetes the inhibitor and drives the enzyme fully into the ES form. The source states that in reversible competitive inhibition ''substrate (S) and inhibitor (I) both bind to the same site on the enzyme... they compete for the active site'' (line 27809), and that ''even in the presence of I, as S increases to infinity, all E is converted to ES. That is, there is no free E to which I could bind... ES = E0; hence v = VM. VM is not changed'' (lines 27829-27830). The text further confirms that ''In the presence of I, VM does not change, but KM appears to increase'' (line 27845), so saturating substrate restores the original maximum velocity. (Choice A) is incorrect: the reaction does not halt, because at infinite S the enzyme is fully saturated and turns over at v = VM, not zero (lines 27829-27830). (Choice B) is incorrect: for a competitive inhibitor the apparent Km rises, not falls, since ''KM appears to increase'' (line 27845) and ''KMapp = KM(1+I/Kis)'' (lines 27869-27870); a Km that drops below normal is the uncompetitive signature (''decreasing the Kmapp,'' line 27120... line 28120). (Choice C) is incorrect: a permanently reduced Vmax with unchanged Km is the noncompetitive outcome, where ''the apparent Vm, Vmapp, is less than the real Vm without an inhibitor'' while ''the apparent Km, Kmapp, will not change'' (lines 28240-28242); this does not apply to competitive inhibition, whose Vmax is unchanged. (Choice D) is correct: because inhibitor and substrate compete for the same active site, saturating substrate converts all enzyme to ES so that v = VM, and the original uninhibited Vmax is reached (lines 27829-27830, 27845). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of competitive inhibition to predict how saturating substrate shifts the E + S equilibrium and restores the maximum velocity, while distinguishing this outcome from the noncompetitive Vmax-drop trap.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
