-- Q5 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A pharmacologist characterizes a small molecule that slows a metabolic enzyme without ever competing with substrate for binding: the compound can attach to the free enzyme and to the enzyme-substrate complex with equal affinity, and adding excess substrate does not relieve the inhibition. Based on this behavior, this noncompetitive inhibitor most likely binds at which location on the enzyme?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "An allosteric site distinct from the active site"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The active site, where substrate binds"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Only the enzyme-substrate (ES) complex"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The substrate itself rather than the enzyme"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because a noncompetitive inhibitor binds at an allosteric site that is separate from the active site. The reference states that ''reversible noncompetitive inhibition occurs when I binds to both E and ES'' with ''the dissociation constants of I for E and ES... the same'' (line 28225-28226); an inhibitor that can bind the ES complex (in which substrate already occupies the active site) cannot itself be sitting in the active site, so it must occupy a different, allosteric location. The text defines this as ''an allosteric (other) binding site,'' a ''site different from the binding site for the main ligand'' (lines 20522-20525), and notes that an inhibitor at such a site ''does not compete with'' the substrate (line 19519). Because it does not occupy the active site, excess substrate cannot displace it, so \(V_{max}\) falls while \(K_m\) is unchanged (line 28241-28242). (Choice A) is correct: a noncompetitive inhibitor binds an allosteric site distinct from the active site, which is why it can bind both E and ES and is not relieved by substrate (lines 28225-28226, 20525). (Choice B) is incorrect: binding the active site ''where substrate binds'' describes competitive inhibition, in which substrate and inhibitor ''compete for the active site and bind in a mutually exclusive fashion'' (line 27809-27810) and inhibition IS relieved by excess substrate — the opposite of the described behavior. (Choice C) is incorrect: binding ''only to ES and not free E'' is the signature of uncompetitive inhibition (line 28099), whereas the described inhibitor binds both free E and ES with equal affinity. (Choice D) is incorrect: the inhibitor acts on the enzyme, not the substrate; the active site is ''the part of an enzyme that directly binds to a substrate'' (line 5268-5269), so the substrate is the species that occupies the active site, not a target that an inhibitor binds. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a noncompetitive inhibitor binds an allosteric site distinct from the active site, allowing it to bind both free enzyme and the enzyme-substrate complex.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 45)
  RETURNING id
)
