-- Q7 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A researcher characterizing an enzyme finds that a reversible inhibitor lowers \(V_{max}\) while leaving \(K_m\) unchanged, consistent with the inhibitor binding equally to both free enzyme and the enzyme–substrate complex at a site distinct from the active site. If the researcher then runs the assay at progressively higher substrate concentrations, the inhibition would most likely be', '[{"label": "A", "text": "fully relieved, because excess substrate outcompetes the inhibitor for binding."}, {"label": "B", "text": "relieved only at low substrate concentrations, where the inhibitor cannot bind."}, {"label": "C", "text": "not relieved, because the inhibitor binds a separate site and substrate cannot displace it."}, {"label": "D", "text": "relieved, because raising the substrate concentration also raises the assay temperature."}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because the described inhibitor is a noncompetitive inhibitor: it binds both free enzyme and the ES complex at a site separate from the active site, so substrate and inhibitor are not competing for the same site and adding more substrate cannot displace it. The text states that in noncompetitive inhibition the inhibitor ''binds to both E and ES,'' and that ''as S increases to infinity, not all of E is converted to ES—there is a finite amount of ESI, even at infinite S,'' so ''the apparent Vm, Vmapp, is less than the real Vm'' (lines 28225, 28239–28241). Because the inhibitor occupies a distinct site and the ESI dead-end complex persists even at saturating substrate, the inhibition cannot be overcome by adding substrate; this is why \(V_{max}\) falls while \(K_m\) is ''unchanged'' (line 28554). (Choice A) is wrong because being overcome by excess substrate is the hallmark of competitive inhibition, not noncompetitive: the summary states ''competitive inhibitors can be overcome by increasing substrate concentration in vitro'' (lines 28564–28565), since for a competitive inhibitor ''as S increases to infinity, all E is converted to ES… v = VM'' (lines 27829–27831); a noncompetitive inhibitor binds a separate site, so substrate cannot outcompete it. (Choice B) is wrong because the noncompetitive inhibitor binds free enzyme and ES with equal affinity regardless of substrate level, so relief does not occur selectively at low substrate; \(K_m\) is unchanged precisely because the inhibitor ''binds to both E and ES with the same affinity'' (line 28242). (Choice C) is correct: the noncompetitive inhibitor binds a site distinct from the active site—it ''does not enter into the pocket where the substrate binds'' (line 28356)—so substrate cannot displace it and the inhibition persists even at high substrate. (Choice D) is wrong because raising substrate concentration does not raise assay temperature, and temperature is a separate variable that, in excess, causes ''irreversible denaturation'' rather than relieving a reversible inhibitor (lines 28568–28569). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that noncompetitive inhibitors bind a separate site on both E and ES and therefore cannot be overcome by adding substrate, unlike competitive inhibitors.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
