-- Q8 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A team assays a metabolic enzyme across a range of substrate concentrations, first alone and then with a candidate inhibitor held at a fixed concentration. Fitting each data set to the Michaelis-Menten model, they find that the inhibitor leaves \(V_{max}\) essentially unchanged while the apparent \(K_m\) roughly doubles. Which mode of inhibition is most consistent with this kinetic signature?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Noncompetitive inhibition"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Uncompetitive inhibition"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Irreversible inhibition"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Competitive inhibition"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because an unchanged \(V_{max}\) paired with an increased apparent \(K_m\) is the defining kinetic signature of competitive inhibition: the source states that with a competitive inhibitor ''VM does not change, but KM appears to increase'' (line 27845). Mechanistically, the inhibitor and substrate ''both bind to the same site on the enzyme... they compete for the active site'' (line 27809), so as \([S]\) rises to infinity all enzyme is driven into ES, no free E remains for I to bind, and ''VM is not changed''; meanwhile I binding to free E shifts E + S \(\rightarrow\) ES to the left, which ''would increase the KMapp'' (lines 27830-27832). Only a signature that raises \(K_m\) yet spares \(V_{max}\) fits, and that is unique to the competitive case. (Choice A) is wrong because noncompetitive inhibition lowers \(V_{max}\) while leaving \(K_m\) unchanged: ''the apparent Vm... is less than the real Vm... the apparent Km, Kmapp, will not change'' (lines 28241-28242) and ''just Vm will decrease'' (line 28244) — the observed unchanged \(V_{max}\) rules it out. (Choice B) is wrong because uncompetitive inhibition, where I ''binds only to ES and not free E'' (line 28099), decreases both parameters: ''In the presence of I, both Vm and Km decrease'' (line 28121), so it cannot leave \(V_{max}\) unchanged and would lower rather than raise \(K_m\). (Choice C) is wrong because the three reversible types modeled here are, by definition, reversible — ''Reversible Competitive inhibition'' (line 27809) — and irreversible inhibition is not characterized by this reversible, substrate-relievable \(V_{max}\)-unchanged/\(K_m\)-increased pattern; it is a distinct category that does not produce the described signature. (Choice D) is correct: an unchanged \(V_{max}\) with an elevated apparent \(K_m\) matches competitive inhibition exactly, per line 27845. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic \(K_m\)/\(V_{max}\) signatures to classify the inhibition type directly from the assay data rather than recall a single definition.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
