-- Q9 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A researcher characterizing a small-molecule inhibitor observes that it only slows the enzyme after substrate has already bound, and it appears to lock the substrate in place rather than blocking its entry. Based on this behavior, this uncompetitive inhibitor most likely binds to which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Only the enzyme–substrate (ES) complex"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Only the free enzyme"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The active site of the free enzyme"}, {"label": "D", "text": "An allosteric site on the free enzyme"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because an uncompetitive inhibitor binds exclusively to the enzyme–substrate complex and not to the free enzyme. The source states, ''Reversible uncompetitive inhibition occurs when I binds only to ES and not free E'' (line 28099), and a learning objective describes uncompetitive inhibition as the case ''where the inhibitor binds only to the enzyme–substrate (ES) complex'' (line 27758). The text further explains that ''on binding S, a conformational change in E occurs, which presents a binding site for I'' and that the resulting ESI is ''a dead-end complex that has only one fate: to return to ES'' (lines 28099–28101), consistent with locking the substrate in place. Because the inhibitor can only act after the substrate is bound, both the apparent \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) decrease proportionally (lines 28121–28123). (Choice A) is correct: uncompetitive inhibition is defined by binding solely to the ES complex, matching the observation that inhibition requires prior substrate binding. (Choice B) is wrong because binding ''only to E, the free enzyme'' describes competitive inhibition, in which ''I binds to E alone and not ES'' (line 27831); this would not require the substrate to bind first. (Choice C) is wrong because binding the active site of the free enzyme is competitive inhibition, where ''substrate (S) and inhibitor (I) both bind to the same site on the enzyme... they compete for the active site'' (lines 27809–27810), the opposite of requiring an ES complex. (Choice D) is wrong because binding an allosteric site on the free enzyme describes an inhibitor that acts on free E (as in allosteric competitive or noncompetitive inhibition, which ''binds to both E and ES,'' line 28225), whereas an uncompetitive inhibitor does not bind free enzyme at all. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining binding target of an uncompetitive inhibitor.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
