-- Q10 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A researcher studying a metabolic enzyme adds a small molecule that binds only to the enzyme–substrate (ES) complex and never to free enzyme, forming a dead-end ESI complex. If she then constructs a Michaelis–Menten curve in the presence of this inhibitor, how would the apparent \(K_m\) and apparent \(V_{max}\) most likely compare to the uninhibited values?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Apparent \\(K_m\\) and apparent \\(V_{max}\\) both increase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Apparent \\(K_m\\) and apparent \\(V_{max}\\) both decrease"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Apparent \\(V_{max}\\) decreases while apparent \\(K_m\\) is unchanged"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Apparent \\(K_m\\) increases while apparent \\(V_{max}\\) is unchanged"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because an inhibitor that binds only to the ES complex and not to free enzyme is an uncompetitive inhibitor, and the text states that such an inhibitor is ''reducing both VM and KM proportionally'' (line 28550). Mechanistically, because I binds to ES alone, it pulls the E + S ↔ ES equilibrium to the right, so the text notes it ''would have the effect of decreasing the Kmapp (i.e., it would appear that the affinity of E and S has increased)'' (line 28120), while the diversion of enzyme into a nonproductive ESI complex means ''the apparent Vm, Vmapp is less than the real Vm without the inhibitor'' (line 28118). The rearranged rate law confirms both fall together: ''Kmapp = Km/(1+I/Kii) and Vmapp = Vm/(1+I/Kii)'' (lines 28151–28152), so both apparent parameters decrease. (Choice A) is wrong because neither parameter increases; both the apparent \(K_m\) and apparent \(V_{max}\) drop, as the source specifies ''both Vm and Km decrease'' (line 28121). (Choice B) is correct: uncompetitive inhibitors bind exclusively to ES, and the text confirms this reduces ''both VM and KM proportionally'' (line 28550), producing the parallel Lineweaver–Burk lines that are the hallmark of this mechanism. (Choice C) is wrong because a decrease in \(V_{max}\) with an unchanged \(K_m\) is the signature of pure noncompetitive inhibition, where I binds both E and ES equally so that ''VM decreases without changing KM'' (line 28554); an ES-only binder instead lowers \(K_m\) as well. (Choice D) is wrong because an increased \(K_m\) with an unchanged \(V_{max}\) is the signature of competitive inhibition, where substrate and inhibitor ''compete for the same active site'' and ''VM remains unchanged, the apparent KM increases'' (lines 28546–28547); an uncompetitive inhibitor does not bind the active site and lowers, rather than raises, both parameters. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanistic definition of ES-only binding to predict its coupled effect on both apparent kinetic constants and distinguish it from the competitive and noncompetitive signatures.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
