-- Q11 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A biochemist studies a small molecule that inhibits a metabolic enzyme by binding at a site distinct from the active site. Kinetic assays show that the compound lowers \(V_{max}\) and also shifts the apparent \(K_m\), and equilibrium-binding studies reveal that it associates with the free enzyme and with the enzyme-substrate complex at measurably different dissociation constants. Which feature most directly identifies this compound as a mixed inhibitor rather than a classical noncompetitive inhibitor?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It binds only to the free enzyme and cannot associate with the enzyme-substrate complex."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It binds only to the enzyme-substrate complex and cannot associate with the free enzyme."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It binds both the free enzyme and the enzyme-substrate complex, but with different affinities for each."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It binds directly within the active site and competes with substrate for that site."}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because a mixed inhibitor binds to both the free enzyme (E) and the enzyme-substrate complex (ES) but with unequal affinities for the two forms. The source states that ''noncompetitive inhibition occurs when I binds to both E and ES,'' that noncompetitive is the special case ''in which the dissociation constants of I for E and ES are the same,'' and that ''in the more general case, the Kd''s are different, and the inhibition is called mixed'' (lines 28225-28229). The learning objectives reinforce this, distinguishing ''noncompetitive inhibition (where the inhibitor binds to both free enzyme and ES with equal affinity) and mixed inhibition (with differing affinities)'' (lines 27763-27764). Because a mixed inhibitor perturbs the E + S equilibrium unequally, ''both parameters change in mixed inhibition'' (lines 27765-27766), matching the observed drop in \(V_{max}\) and shift in \(K_m\). (Choice A) is wrong: binding only the free enzyme, not ES, describes competitive inhibition, in which ''I binds to E alone and not ES'' and ''they compete for the active site'' (lines 27809, 27831), and competitive inhibitors leave \(V_{max}\) unchanged rather than lowering it. (Choice B) is wrong: binding only to the ES complex and not free enzyme defines uncompetitive inhibition, since ''reversible uncompetitive inhibition occurs when I binds only to ES and not free E'' (line 28099). (Choice C) is correct: it binds both the free enzyme and the ES complex with different affinities, which is precisely the ''more general case'' the source labels mixed inhibition, contrasted with noncompetitive in which the dissociation constants for E and ES are equal (lines 28225-28229, 27763-27764). (Choice D) is wrong: binding within the active site to compete with substrate describes competitive inhibition, where ''substrate (S) and inhibitor (I) both bind to the same site on the enzyme'' and ''compete for the active site'' (line 27809), whereas the scenario specifies a site distinct from the active site. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanistic definitions of the reversible inhibition types to interpret the binding and kinetic data and select the single feature that discriminates mixed from noncompetitive inhibition.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
