-- Q12 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A researcher characterizes a reversible mixed inhibitor of a metabolic enzyme and finds that it binds the free enzyme (E) more tightly than the enzyme-substrate complex (ES). Compared with the uninhibited reaction, how would the apparent \(K_m\) and apparent \(V_{max}\) most likely change in the presence of this inhibitor?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Apparent \\(K_m\\) decreases and apparent \\(V_{max}\\) decreases."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Apparent \\(K_m\\) is unchanged and apparent \\(V_{max}\\) is unchanged."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Apparent \\(K_m\\) increases and apparent \\(V_{max}\\) is unchanged."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Apparent \\(K_m\\) increases and apparent \\(V_{max}\\) decreases."}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because a mixed inhibitor binds both free enzyme (E) and the ES complex, and the source gives the apparent parameters as \(K_{m,app} = K_m(1+I/K_{is})/(1+I/K_{ii})\) and \(V_{m,app} = V_m/(1+I/K_{ii})\), where \(K_{is}\) is the dissociation constant for binding to free E and \(K_{ii}\) is the dissociation constant for binding to ES (libretexts-biochemistry.txt line 28253: ''Kmapp = Km(1+I/Kis)/(1+I/Kii) = Km when Kis=Kii, and Vmapp = Vm/(1+I/Kii)''). Because the inhibitor binds free E more tightly, \(K_{is} < K_{ii}\), so the factor \((1+I/K_{is})\) exceeds \((1+I/K_{ii})\); their ratio is greater than 1 and the apparent \(K_m\) rises above \(K_m\). The \(K_{ii}\) term in the denominator still makes \(V_{m,app}\) less than \(V_m\), so \(V_{max}\) falls, consistent with the source''s statement that ''mixed inhibition shows changes in both parameters'' (line 28554). (Choice A) is wrong: falling apparent \(K_m\) together with falling \(V_{max}\) is the pattern when the inhibitor instead prefers the ES complex (\(K_{ii} < K_{is}\)), which reverses the required \(K_m\) direction; a decreased \(K_m\) with decreased \(V_{max}\) is the uncompetitive signature (line 28118: ''both Vm and Km decrease''), not the case described. (Choice B) is wrong: unchanged \(K_m\) and \(V_{max}\) describes an uninhibited reaction, but any mixed inhibitor lowers \(V_{max}\) through the \((1+I/K_{ii})\) denominator (line 28254). (Choice C) is wrong: an unchanged \(V_{max}\) with an increased \(K_m\) is the competitive signature, in which the inhibitor binds only free E and ''VM is not changed'' while ''apparent KM ... will change'' (line 28830 region, lines 28830/27845), whereas a mixed inhibitor also binds ES and therefore lowers \(V_{max}\). (Choice D) is correct: preference for free E makes \(K_{is} < K_{ii}\), driving the apparent \(K_m\) up while the ES-binding term lowers apparent \(V_{max}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mixed-inhibition rate equations to a stated binding preference, compare the two dissociation constants, and deduce the resulting directions of both kinetic parameters.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
