-- Q13 [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)', 'A researcher characterizes two reversible inhibitors of a metabolic enzyme by measuring initial-rate kinetics at several fixed inhibitor concentrations. She finds that inhibitor X lowers both the apparent \(V_{max}\) and the apparent \(K_m\), whereas inhibitor Y leaves the apparent \(V_{max}\) unchanged but raises the apparent \(K_m\). Based on these kinetic signatures, how should inhibitors X and Y most likely be classified?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "X is competitive and Y is uncompetitive"}, {"label": "B", "text": "X is noncompetitive and Y is competitive"}, {"label": "C", "text": "X is uncompetitive and Y is competitive"}, {"label": "D", "text": "X is noncompetitive and Y is noncompetitive"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because each inhibitor must be identified from its combined effect on both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\). For inhibitor X, the text states that in uncompetitive inhibition the inhibitor ''binds only to ES and not free E'' and that ''In the presence of I, both Vm and Km decrease'' (lines 28099, 28121-28122), which exactly matches X lowering both parameters. For inhibitor Y, competitive inhibition is defined by the inhibitor and substrate binding ''the same site on the enzyme'' in a ''mutually exclusive fashion,'' where ''VM is not changed. However, the apparent KM, KMapp, will change... increase the KMapp'' (lines 27809-27810, 27830-27832), which matches Y leaving \(V_{max}\) unchanged while raising \(K_m\). Only choice C assigns X to uncompetitive and Y to competitive. (Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the assignments; competitive inhibition leaves \(V_{max}\) unchanged (line 27830-27831), so X (which lowers \(V_{max}\)) cannot be competitive, and uncompetitive inhibition lowers \(V_{max}\) (line 28121-28122), so Y (whose \(V_{max}\) is unchanged) cannot be uncompetitive. (Choice B) is a partial-truth trap: it correctly labels Y as competitive but mislabels X, because in noncompetitive inhibition ''the apparent Km, Kmapp, will not change'' (line 28241-28242), yet X''s \(K_m\) decreases. (Choice C) is correct: X binds only ES so both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) fall (uncompetitive), while Y competes for the active site so \(V_{max}\) is unchanged and \(K_m\) rises (competitive). (Choice D) is incorrect: noncompetitive inhibition requires \(V_{max}\) to decrease with \(K_m\) unchanged (lines 28241-28242), but X shows a decreased \(K_m\) and Y shows an unchanged \(V_{max}\), so neither inhibitor fits the noncompetitive signature. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) signatures of each inhibition mechanism to tabulated kinetic data, classify two inhibitors simultaneously, and reject permuted labelings that satisfy only one of the two required criteria.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
