-- Q14 [Irreversible Inhibition] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Irreversible Inhibition', 'A researcher treats a purified enzyme with a small molecule that chemically modifies an essential cysteine residue in the active site, and finds that the enzyme''s activity does not return even after the mixture is extensively dialyzed and diluted. Which feature best characterizes this type of irreversible inhibition?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The enzyme''s activity is readily restored when the inhibitor is removed by dilution or dialysis."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The inhibition can be overcome simply by adding a large excess of substrate."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The inhibitor only briefly slows the enzyme before spontaneously dissociating from the active site."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The inhibitor permanently inactivates the enzyme, often by covalently modifying an essential active-site residue."}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because irreversible inhibition permanently inactivates the enzyme, typically by covalently modifying an essential side chain in or near the active site so that activity cannot be restored. The LibreTexts text states that ''we could covalently modify certain side chains, which, if they are essential to enzymatic activity, would irreversibly inhibit the enzyme'' (line 27798) and that irreversible inhibitors ''modify key amino acid side chains (e.g., cysteine modification by iodoacetamide) to permanently inactivate an enzyme'' (lines 27745-27746). The chapter summary reinforces that ''enzyme activity can be permanently abolished through irreversible modifications'' via ''covalent binding of small molecules (e.g., iodoacetamide modifying critical cysteine residues)'' (lines 28539-28541). Because the bond is covalent, the enzyme stays inactive even after the free inhibitor is removed. (Choice A) is wrong because ready reversal by dilution or dialysis describes reversible, noncovalent inhibition; the text notes that reversible inhibition is the ''reversible, noncovalent inhibition'' discussed later (line 27806), which is the opposite of a permanent covalent modification. (Choice B) is wrong because being overcome by excess substrate is the signature of competitive (reversible) inhibition, where ''competitive inhibitors can be overcome by increasing substrate concentration'' (lines 28564-28565); a covalently inactivated active site cannot bind substrate no matter how much is added. (Choice C) is wrong because a transient effect that spontaneously reverses is characteristic of weak, noncovalent reversible binding, not the permanent inactivation produced by covalent modification (line 27798). (Choice D) is correct: the inhibitor permanently inactivates the enzyme, often by covalently modifying an essential active-site residue, and this modification is not relieved by dilution or by adding substrate. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining feature of irreversible inhibition—permanent, often covalent, inactivation of the enzyme.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
