-- Q15 [Irreversible Inhibition] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Irreversible Inhibition', 'A researcher observes that a small molecule abolishes the catalytic activity of a purified enzyme. When the treated enzyme is subjected to extensive dialysis and dilution, no activity is recovered, and adding a large excess of substrate likewise fails to restore any measurable turnover. Which classification best accounts for these observations?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Irreversible inhibition, reflecting permanent inactivation of the enzyme"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Competitive inhibition, which excess substrate would be expected to relieve"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Noncompetitive inhibition, which dilution would be expected to relieve"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Allosteric activation, which would raise rather than abolish activity"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because inhibition that cannot be reversed by either removing free inhibitor (dialysis/dilution) or by out-competing it with substrate is the diagnostic signature of irreversible, typically covalent, inactivation. The text states that covalently modifying essential side chains would ''irreversibly inhibit the enzyme'' and that ''enzyme activity can be permanently abolished through irreversible modifications... [that] render the enzyme inactive'' (lines 27797-27798, 28538-28541). Because the modification is permanent, neither diluting away unbound inhibitor nor flooding the active site with substrate can rescue activity, which is exactly what is observed here. All reversible mechanisms, by contrast, are relieved by removing inhibitor or, for active-site competitors, by excess substrate. (Choice A) is correct: the joint failure of dilution and excess substrate to recover any activity uniquely fits permanent, irreversible inactivation. (Choice B) is wrong because competitive inhibition is reversible and, per the text, ''competitive inhibitors can be overcome by increasing substrate concentration'' (line 28565), whereas here excess substrate restores nothing; this is the partial-truth trap, since only one of the two failed tests is even relevant to competitive inhibition. (Choice C) is wrong because ''Reversible noncompetitive inhibition occurs when I binds to both E and ES'' (line 28225) noncovalently, so removing free inhibitor by dialysis/dilution would restore activity, contrary to the observation. (Choice D) is wrong because an allosteric effector can be ''an inhibitor or activator'' (line 21236), and activation would increase rather than abolish activity, which contradicts the reported loss of all turnover. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the reversibility criteria for enzyme inhibition to experimental data and reason from the joint failure of two distinct reversal tests to eliminate every reversible mechanism and the competitive partial-truth trap.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
