-- Q16 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)', 'A researcher characterizing a metabolic enzyme observes that its activity can be turned up by one small molecule and turned down by a different one, even though neither molecule resembles the substrate. Which structural feature best explains how this enzyme can be regulated by such effectors?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It possesses only an active site and no other ligand-binding regions"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It possesses one or more regulatory (allosteric) sites that are distinct from the active site"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It lacks an active site, so effectors bind wherever substrate would otherwise bind"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It is a single, unregulated polypeptide that binds only its substrate"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because an allosteric enzyme has, in addition to the active (orthosteric) site where substrate binds, one or more separate allosteric sites where activators or inhibitors bind. The reference states that in heterotropic (Type II) allosterism ''a chemical modulator binds to a site different from the binding site for the main ligand'' and that ''The modulator binds at an allosteric (other) binding site,'' which then ''modulates (activates or inhibits) the binding affinity of the main ligand for the active or orthosteric site'' (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 20522–20525). The Learning Goals for this section likewise emphasize the need to ''distinguish between allosteric binding at orthosteric versus allosteric sites'' (line 20507), and hemoglobin illustrates the same principle by binding H+, CO2, and bisphosphoglycerate ''at sites (allosteric) distant from the oxygen binding site'' (line 20133). Because the activator and inhibitor in this scenario do not resemble the substrate and bind separately from it, the enzyme must have regulatory sites distinct from its active site. (Choice A) is wrong: an enzyme with only an active site and no other ligand-binding regions would have nowhere for effectors that do not resemble the substrate to bind, yet the reference describes modulators binding at an allosteric site ''different from the binding site for the main ligand'' (lines 20522–20523). (Choice B) is correct: the defining feature of an allosteric enzyme is a regulatory (allosteric) site that is separate from the active/orthosteric site, where activators or inhibitors bind to modulate activity (lines 20522–20525; 20507). (Choice C) is wrong: allosteric enzymes still have a functional active (orthosteric) site where substrate binds—''the main ligand... binds to the same active site... This site is called the orthosteric binding site'' (line 20513)—so it is false that the enzyme lacks an active site. (Choice D) is wrong: the scenario explicitly involves an enzyme whose activity is turned up and down by effectors, and the reference describes allosteric enzymes as being activated or inhibited by modulators (lines 20522–20525) and multimeric allosteric enzymes such as aspartate transcarbamylase that are feedback-regulated (lines 21016, 21091), so an unregulated single polypeptide that binds only substrate does not describe an allosteric enzyme. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that an allosteric enzyme is defined by having one or more regulatory (allosteric) sites separate from its active site.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
