-- Q17 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)', 'A structural biologist observes that a small molecule binds a metabolic enzyme at a site distinct from the active site and increases the enzyme''s catalytic rate. If this molecule is acting as an allosteric activator, which of the following most likely describes its effect on the enzyme?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It physically occupies the active site so that substrate cannot bind."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It permanently inactivates the enzyme by covalently modifying an active-site residue."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It shifts the enzyme toward its active conformation, making the active site more available for substrate binding."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It proteolytically cleaves the enzyme to convert it into an active form."}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because an allosteric activator binds a modulator at a site topographically distant from the active (orthosteric) site and engenders a conformational change that stabilizes the active conformation of the enzyme (line 31119-31121: "Allosteric regulation occurs through binding a modulator... at an allosteric site to engender a conformational change that affects function at the orthosteric site. This effect may cause the re-distribution of the conformational ensemble by either stabilizing an active conformation (allosteric activation) or destabilizing an inactive conformation (allosteric inhibition)"). By favoring the active (R) state, the activator makes the active site more available for substrate binding (line 21476-21477: "binding of allosteric activators (like fructose 1,6-bisphosphate)... shifts the equilibrium to the active or inactive state"; line 21111-21112: "High levels of the substrate... shift the equilibrium to the active R state"). (Choice A) is wrong because physically occupying and blocking the active site so that substrate cannot bind describes a competitive inhibitor, not an activator (line 27809-27810: "Reversible Competitive inhibition occurs when substrate (S) and inhibitor (I) both bind to the same site on the enzyme. In effect, they compete for the active site"). (Choice B) is wrong because permanent inactivation by covalently modifying an essential residue describes irreversible covalent inhibition, which abolishes activity rather than increasing it (line 27797-27798: "we could covalently modify certain side chains, which, if they are essential to enzymatic activity, would irreversibly inhibit the enzyme"). (Choice C) is correct: the activator stabilizes the active conformation, shifting the equilibrium toward the R state and making the active site more available for substrate binding, consistent with the definition of allosteric activation (line 31121). (Choice D) is wrong because proteolytic cleavage to generate an active form describes zymogen activation, a covalent mechanism distinct from reversible allosteric modulation, which acts only by non-covalent binding of a modulator (line 31119: allosteric regulation occurs "through binding a modulator"). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that an allosteric activator binds at a site distant from the active site and stabilizes the enzyme''s active conformation, making the active site more available for substrate.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
