-- Q19 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)', 'A researcher studying a biosynthetic pathway notices that as the final product of the pathway accumulates in the cell, the rate of the pathway''s first, committed reaction falls sharply, even though the concentration of the initial substrate stays high. This behavior is characteristic of feedback (end-product) inhibition. In feedback inhibition, which of the following most accurately describes what is happening?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The end product of the pathway inhibits an enzyme that acts earlier in the same pathway."}, {"label": "B", "text": "An early substrate of the pathway activates the next enzyme downstream of it."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The first enzyme of the pathway inhibits the last enzyme of the pathway."}, {"label": "D", "text": "An unrelated hormone made outside the pathway shuts the entire pathway down."}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because in feedback (end-product) inhibition the final product of a metabolic pathway binds to and inhibits an enzyme that acts earlier in that same pathway. The textbook describes ''feedback inhibition from downstream products'' as a common allosteric regulatory mechanism (line 31126), and gives phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase as ''a classic example of feedback inhibition of the first reaction of a pathway by the end product,'' where ''the end product of the pathway, serine, binds to an allosteric site on PGDH and inhibits it'' (lines 31143-31144). Likewise, for aspartate transcarbamylase, ''the end products of the pathway, CTP and UTP, feed back and allosterically inhibit the enzyme'' (line 21091). This negative feedback lets the cell throttle production once enough end product has been made. (Choice A) is correct: the accumulating end product acting back on an upstream (earlier) enzyme is precisely the definition of feedback inhibition, matching the cited examples of serine inhibiting PGDH and CTP/UTP inhibiting ATCase. (Choice B) is wrong: an early substrate activating a downstream enzyme is feedforward activation, not feedback inhibition; the text explicitly contrasts ''feedback inhibition from downstream products or feedforward from upstream substrates'' (line 31126), and notes that a molecule like ATP can instead act as ''an allosteric activator'' (line 21091), which is the opposite of inhibition. (Choice C) is wrong: it reverses the direction of regulation; in feedback inhibition it is the end product (a small-molecule metabolite), not the first enzyme, that does the inhibiting, and the target is the earlier enzyme, not the last one, as shown by serine inhibiting the first reaction of its pathway (lines 31143-31144). (Choice D) is wrong: the inhibitor is a metabolite of the pathway itself (e.g., serine or CTP/UTP), not an unrelated hormone produced elsewhere; the cited examples all show the pathway''s own end product feeding back on an internal enzyme (lines 21091, 31143-31144). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of feedback (end-product) inhibition and the direction in which the pathway''s final product acts on an earlier enzyme.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
