-- Q20 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)', 'In a biosynthetic pathway, the end product accumulates and binds to an allosteric site on the enzyme that catalyzes the first committed step, inhibiting it. In the context of cellular economy, this feedback inhibition most likely serves which physiological purpose?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "To drive the pathway to synthesize as much end product as rapidly as possible"}, {"label": "B", "text": "To prevent overaccumulation of end product and maintain homeostasis by turning the pathway off once product is sufficient"}, {"label": "C", "text": "To permanently denature the pathway''s enzymes so they can no longer function"}, {"label": "D", "text": "To accelerate every enzymatic step in the pathway simultaneously"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because feedback inhibition is a regulatory mechanism in which a downstream end product binds an allosteric site on an early enzyme to switch its own production off once enough has been made. The source describes serine, the end product of its pathway, binding an allosteric site on PGDH and inhibiting it as ''a classic example of feedback inhibition of the first reaction of a pathway by the end product'' (line 31144), and describes CTP and UTP feeding back to allosterically inhibit ATCase, which ''prevents a buildup of pyrimidine nucleotides'' (lines 21091-21092). Because ''allosteric regulation is particularly important in the cell''s ability to adjust enzyme activity based on the surrounding environmental conditions'' via ''feedback control loops...from downstream products'' (lines 31125-31127), the pathway is dialed down when product is plentiful, conserving resources and maintaining internal homeostasis (line 973). (Choice A) is wrong because feedback inhibition does the opposite of maximizing output: rising end product inhibits the enzyme rather than driving faster synthesis (line 31144). (Choice B) is correct: it prevents overaccumulation of end product and maintains homeostasis by shutting the pathway off when product is sufficient, exactly as CTP/UTP feedback ''prevents a buildup'' of nucleotides (lines 21091-21092). (Choice C) is wrong because feedback inhibition acts by reversible allosteric binding that produces a conformational change (lines 31119-31121), not by permanent denaturation; the enzyme is transiently inhibited, not destroyed. (Choice D) is wrong because feedback inhibition slows the pathway at the first committed step (line 31144) rather than accelerating every step; accelerating all steps would describe activation, not inhibition. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of end-product feedback inhibition to infer its physiological purpose, reasoning from why a cell would allosterically shut down its own biosynthetic pathway once product is abundant rather than simply recalling a definition.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
