-- Q22 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] medium skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)', 'A cell biologist observes that a metabolic enzyme''s catalytic activity is switched off when one signaling pathway is triggered and switched back on when a second, opposing pathway is triggered, and that this on/off cycling depends on the balance of two classes of enzymes acting on the target protein. This behavior is most consistent with regulation by covalent modification. Which of the following mechanisms is most likely responsible?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Reversible binding of the substrate at the active site of the enzyme"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Reversible binding of an allosteric activator at a site distinct from the active site"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A shift in the ambient temperature surrounding the enzyme"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Phosphorylation by a kinase and dephosphorylation by a phosphatase"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because covalent modification of an enzyme by phosphorylation and dephosphorylation reversibly activates or inactivates it, and this cycling is governed by two opposing classes of enzymes. The textbook states that ''protein phosphorylation, is the reversible addition of a phosphoryl group from adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to amino acid side chains such as serine, threonine, and tyrosine residues... This modification causes conformational changes that either (1) affect the catalytic activity to activate or inactivate the protein'' (line 30835-30837), and that ''The reversibility of protein phosphorylation is attributed to the actions of kinases and phosphatases, which phosphorylate and dephosphorylate substrates, respectively'' (line 30842-30843). This is a true covalent change: ''A protein kinase is an enzyme that covalently transfers a terminal phosphate (P) from ATP to a protein'' (line 347) and ''Common covalent protein PTMs include phosphorylation'' (line 30819). (Choice A) is wrong because substrate binding at the active site is a non-covalent binding event, and the textbook explicitly notes that ''substrate-induced changes in the active site are not allosteric since the conformational changes... are caused by binding at the active site'' (line 31158-31160) — no covalent bond is formed. (Choice B) is wrong because allosteric regulation ''occurs through binding a modulator (e.g., small molecule or protein) at an allosteric site'' (line 31119); this reversible binding is non-covalent and is contrasted with covalent phosphorylation (line 30698), so it is not covalent modification. (Choice C) is wrong because a change in temperature alters reaction rate or denatures the protein but does not chemically attach or remove any group from a side chain, and it is not listed among covalent modifications (line 30817-30819). (Choice D) is correct: phosphorylation by a kinase and dephosphorylation by a phosphatase is the reversible covalent on/off switch described in the passage (line 30835-30843). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that covalent modification of enzymes is carried out by the reversible addition and removal of a phosphoryl group by kinases and phosphatases.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
