-- Q23 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)', 'An enzymologist finds that a metabolic enzyme''s activity is switched off when a protein kinase attaches a phosphoryl group to a serine residue, and is switched back on when a phosphatase removes that same group. Based on this behavior, the regulation of this enzyme is best classified as which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Reversible covalent modification"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Allosteric regulation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Irreversible covalent inhibition"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Zymogen activation"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because the enzyme is being toggled by the enzymatic attachment and removal of an actual chemical group (a phosphoryl group), which is the defining feature of reversible covalent modification. The textbook states that ''protein phosphorylation, is the reversible addition of a phosphoryl group from adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to amino acid side chains such as serine, threonine, and tyrosine residues'' and that ''the reversibility of protein phosphorylation is attributed to the actions of kinases and phosphatases, which phosphorylate and dephosphorylate substrates, respectively.'' Because a phosphate group is physically bonded to (and then cleaved from) a side chain, this is a covalent post-translational modification, and the described kinase-on/phosphatase-off behavior maps exactly onto its reversibility. (Choice A) is correct: the kinase covalently attaches a phosphoryl group and the phosphatase removes it, so the switch is a reversible covalent modification. (Choice B) is wrong because allosteric regulation works by the noncovalent binding of a small molecule, ion, or protein ''at sites distinct from the active site'' that ''induce conformational changes'' — no chemical group is covalently attached; here a phosphate is enzymatically bonded to the side chain, so the mechanism is covalent, not merely allosteric binding. (Choice C) is wrong because irreversible covalent inhibition permanently inactivates the enzyme by covalently modifying an essential side chain such that activity cannot be restored; the described enzyme is switched back on by a phosphatase, so the modification is reversible, not irreversible. (Choice D) is wrong because zymogen activation is a one-way process in which an inactive precursor ''become[s] activated on limited proteolysis'' — a permanent peptide-bond cleavage — rather than a phosphoryl group being added and later removed to toggle activity on and off. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of covalent modification, allosteric binding, irreversible inhibition, and proteolytic zymogen activation to the specific kinase/phosphatase behavior described and select the classification whose mechanism matches the evidence.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
