-- Q24 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)', 'A pancreatic acinar cell synthesizes trypsinogen, an inactive precursor that is stored in secretory granules and only becomes catalytically active once it reaches the duodenum. A student proposes that the conversion of trypsinogen to active trypsin is what accounts for this delayed activity. Which mechanism most likely converts this zymogen into its active form?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Reversible phosphorylation of a serine residue by a protein kinase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Binding of a small-molecule allosteric activator at a site distant from the active site"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Proteolytic cleavage that irreversibly removes part of the inactive precursor''s polypeptide"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A simple decrease in the surrounding pH without any change to the polypeptide"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because a zymogen (proenzyme) is an inactive precursor that becomes active only after a covalent biochemical change to its polypeptide: the reference states that ''A zymogen, also called a proenzyme, is an inactive precursor of an enzyme. A zymogen requires a biochemical change (such as a hydrolysis reaction revealing the active site or changing the configuration to reveal the active site) to become an active enzyme'' (line 31299-31300). For trypsinogen specifically, ''enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen by removing the 7-10 amino acids trypsinogen activation peptide (TAP) from the N-terminal region'' and ''Removal of TAP induces a conformational change, resulting in active trypsin'' (lines 31319-31326). The chapter summary reinforces that ''zymogen activation, through specific proteolytic cleavage or conformational changes, ensures that enzymes such as digestive proteases or caspases become active only under appropriate conditions'' (lines 31629-31630), and the analogous chymotrypsinogen ''loses the first 15 amino acids by selective proteolysis'' upon activation (lines 31094-31095, i.e., line 30094-30095). (Choice A) is incorrect: phosphorylation is a post-translational modification, one of the reversible PTMs that ''include phosphorylation, acetylation, glycosylation, ubiquitination'' (line 31613); it is treated as a distinct layer of regulation from zymogen activation and does not describe the irreversible proteolytic maturation of a proenzyme. (Choice B) is incorrect: allosteric regulation involves ''binding events at sites distant from an enzyme''s active site'' that ''induce conformational changes that modulate activity'' (lines 31622-31624); this reversible ligand binding is a separate regulatory mechanism and does not cleave the inactive precursor. (Choice C) is correct: activation of a zymogen occurs by proteolytic cleavage that irreversibly removes part of the precursor polypeptide, as when enteropeptidase excises the trypsinogen activation peptide to yield active trypsin (lines 31299-31300, 31319-31326). (Choice D) is incorrect: the reference specifies that activation requires an enzymatic ''biochemical change (such as a hydrolysis reaction revealing the active site)'' carried out by a protease such as enteropeptidase (lines 31299-31300, 31319); a mere change in pH does not cleave the polypeptide and is not the described activation mechanism. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that zymogens are inactive proenzymes activated by irreversible proteolytic cleavage rather than by reversible modifications such as phosphorylation, allosteric binding, or pH change.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
