-- Q25 [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)', 'A researcher studies a secreted digestive protease that is stored as an inactive precursor. Activation occurs only after a second protease snips off an N-terminal fragment, and once that fragment is removed the enzyme stays active — the cell has no way to turn it off by simply undoing the activating step. The researcher contrasts this behavior with a cytoplasmic enzyme whose activity is toggled on and off by a kinase and its opposing phosphatase. The most defensible statement about how the protease''s activation differs from the kinase-based control is that:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "both mechanisms are freely reversible, so each enzyme can be switched between its active and inactive states as needed"}, {"label": "B", "text": "the proteolytic activation is essentially irreversible, whereas the phosphorylation-based control is reversible"}, {"label": "C", "text": "the proteolytic activation is reversible, whereas the phosphorylation-based control is irreversible"}, {"label": "D", "text": "the two mechanisms are functionally identical, since both simply add a chemical group to switch the enzyme on"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because zymogen activation proceeds by cleaving a peptide bond, and a cell cannot re-form the severed bond to reverse the change, whereas phosphorylation is undone by a phosphatase. A zymogen ''requires a biochemical change (such as a hydrolysis reaction revealing the active site...) to become an active enzyme'' (line 31299-31300), and upon activation of chymotrypsinogen ''it loses the first 15 amino acids by selective proteolysis'' (line 30094-30095) — a permanent loss, so the activation is essentially irreversible. In contrast, phosphorylation is a covalent modification added by ''kinases'' and removed by ''phosphatases'' (line 6248-6250), making it a reversible on/off cycle. Because the scenario states the enzyme ''stays active'' and cannot be turned off by undoing the cleavage, the discriminating feature is reversibility, not the mere presence of covalent chemistry. (Choice A) is wrong: proteolytic cleavage is not freely reversible; the excised fragment is lost and the peptide bond cannot be rejoined by the cell, so the enzyme cannot be switched back off this way (line 30094-30095). (Choice B) is correct: proteolytic (zymogen) activation is essentially irreversible because it destroys a covalent peptide bond, while phosphorylation is reversible via the kinase/phosphatase cycle (lines 31299-31300, 6248-6250). (Choice C) reverses the true relationship: it is the proteolysis that is irreversible and the phosphorylation that is reversible, not the other way around (lines 30094-30095, 6248-6250). (Choice D) is wrong: the two are not identical, and proteolysis removes a peptide fragment rather than adding a chemical group; only phosphorylation adds a group (a phosphate from ATP), and only it is reversible (lines 31299-31300, 6248-6250). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between bond-cleaving and group-transfer regulation to judge which mechanism can be undone by the cell and which cannot.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 90)
  RETURNING id
)
